## Section 1 — Input Sanitization

### 🟠 HIGH — S1-01: No Server-Side Message Length Cap on Chat Messages

**File:** `lib/chat_screen.dart` (L81–89), `lib/support_chat_screen.dart` (L82–87)  
**Evidence:** Both chat screens write directly to Firestore with no maximum length on the `text` field:
```dart
// chat_screen.dart L81-89
await FirebaseService.firestore
    .collection('chats')
    .doc(widget.chatId)
    .collection('messages')
    .add({
  'text': text,           // ← No maxLength enforced before write
  'senderId': user.uid,
  'createdAt': FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),
});
```
The UI `TextField` in `chat_screen.dart` has `maxLines: 3` which is a soft UI constraint only. An attacker using the Firebase SDK directly can write megabytes per message.

**Impact:** A single malicious user can write ~1 MB messages in a loop, causing:
1. Firestore document reads that are billed per 4 KB. One 1 MB message = 250 Firestore read operations per viewer.
2. Potential Firestore document size violations (1 MB document limit) which silently corrupt the conversation.
3. Storage of attacker-controlled HTML/script content displayed in the mobile UI (not XSS in Flutter native, but visible to admin in any web-based admin portal).

**Fix Required:** Add a server-side Firestore rule to cap `text` field size, e.g.:
```
request.resource.data.text is string
&& request.resource.data.text.size() <= 2000
```

